WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.573
Risk wants payments that are large for their own merchant.

2
00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:14.127
550 cents is tiny for Volt Electronics, but twice a Metro Transit fare of 275.

3
00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:19.822
Comparing with the average of all payments, 24,015, misses it.

4
00:00:19.822 --> 00:00:22.389
Metro's 550 is never flagged.

5
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:27.415
So the subquery must use the outer row: average only this row's merchant.

6
00:00:27.415 --> 00:00:30.976
A subquery that refers to the outer row is correlated.

7
00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:37.160
It can't run until there's an outer row, so it runs once per row.

8
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:45.182
For payment 3, it reads all 37 payments, keeps Volt's seven, and averages them: 48,621.

9
00:00:45.182 --> 00:00:48.317
129,900 is above, so it's flagged.

10
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:50.534
Payment 4 is Metro's.

11
00:00:50.534 --> 00:00:57.227
The subquery runs again from scratch: average 344, and 275 is below.

12
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:03.465
Payment 9 is Metro's too, and it computes the same 344 all over again.

13
00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:10.820
After all 30 payments: 30 runs and 1,110 rows read, for six different answers.

14
00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:21.344
On the large data set, one run takes 51 milliseconds, and there are 930,826 payments: about 13 hours.

15
00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:22.571
Turn it around.

16
00:01:22.571 --> 00:01:26.859
One pass with GROUP BY computes every merchant's average once.

17
00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:34.997
A second pass joins each payment to its merchant's average: 282 milliseconds instead of 13 hours.

18
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.835
Some planners do this rewrite on their own.

19
00:01:37.835 --> 00:01:43.533
PostgreSQL does it for EXISTS and IN, but not for an average, so you write the join yourself.

20
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:47.175
A subquery that names the outer row runs once per row.

21
00:01:47.175 --> 00:01:50.552
Compute the per-merchant answer once, and join to it.
